The domination polynomial of \\(G\\cong G(n_{1},\\dots,n_{t})\\) is \\[D(G,x)=((1+x)^{n_{1}}-1)((1+x)^{n-n_{1}}-1)+((1+x)^{n_{1}}-1)+\\prod_{i=2}^{t}((1+x)^{n_{i}}-1).\\]\nL197: \nL198: What can be said about the domination zeros and their limits, log-concave and unimodal property of \\(D(G,x)?\\) This problem can be investigated in future.\nL199: The vertex connectivity of a graph \\(G\\), denoted by \\(\\kappa(G)\\), is the minimum number of vertices of \\(G\\) whose deletion disconnects \\(G\\).\nL200: Let \\(\\mathcal{F}_n\\) be the family of simple connected graphs on \\(n\\) vertices and let \\[\\mathcal{V}_n^k =\\Big\\{G\\in\\mathcal{F}_n:\\kappa(G)\\leq k \\Big\\},\\] that is, \\(\\mathcal{V}_n^k\\) is the family of graphs with vertex connectivity at most \\(k.\\) The graphs in the family \\(\\mathcal{V}_n^k\\) are \\(K_{k}+(K_{t}\\cup K_{n-t-k}),\\) for connectivity \\(k=1,2,\\dots,n-1\\) and \\(t\\leq n-k-t,\\) which implies that \\(t \\leq \\left \\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{2}\\right \\rfloor.\\) From Theorem 2.16, we have the following consequence.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRoot geometry of domination polynomials for friendship and book graphs (https://arxiv.org/html/2604.08998v1)\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"lineno\":420}); Total lines: 515\nL396: Figure 【56†5】 visualizes the three pieces appearing in Theorem 【52†5.1】. The circle $|x+2|=1$, the hyperbola $(a+1)^{2}-b^{2}=\\tfrac{1}{2}$, the quartic curve $|x+1|^{2}=|x|$, and the special points $0$ and $-\\tfrac{1}{2}$. The actual limit set consists only of the portions of these curves satisfying the dominance conditions defining $C_{12}$, $C_{13}$, and $C_{23}$.\nL397: It presents a geometric representation of the book-graph limit set, demonstrating the formation of its boundary by the intersection of circular, hyperbolic, and quartic algebraic curves within the complex plane.\nL398: ## 6 Question 3.6: Integer domination roots\nL399: \nL400: This section gives a partial affirmative answer to the integer-root problem for the graph families studied in [【29†7】].\nL401: ###### Theorem 6.1.\nL402: \nL403: For every $n\\geq 1$, the friendship graph $F_{n}$ has no nonzero integer domination root.\nL404: \nL405: Proof. If $n$ is odd, then [【29†7】] showed that $F_{n}$ has no nonzero real domination root. Hence it has no nonzero integer domination root. Assume now that $n$ is even. By Theorem 【39†3.1】, the only real roots of $D(F_{n},x)$ are $0,x_{n}^{-}\\in(-2,-1),$ and $x_{n}^{+}\\in(-1,0).$ Neither interval contains an integer. Therefore, $0$ is the only integer domination root of $F_{n}$. ∎\nL406: ###### Theorem 6.2.\nL407: \nL408: For every $n\\geq 1$, the book graph $B_{n}$ has no nonzero integer domination root.\nL409: \nL410: Proof. For every positive integer $m$, the definition of domination polynomial shows that $D(B_{n},m)>0,$ since all coefficients count dominating sets and are therefore nonnegative, with at least one positive coefficient. Hence no positive integer can be a root. We now validate negative integers. First, observe that\nL411:  | $$D(B_{n},-1)=(-1)(-1+2)^{n}+1\\cdot 0^{2n}-2(-1)^{n}=\\begin{cases}-3,&n\\text{ even},\\\\\nL412: 1,&n\\text{ odd},\\end{cases}$$  |\nL413: \nL414: so it implies that $-1$ is never its root. Furthermore, $D(B_{n},-2)=4-2(-2)^{n},$ which is never zero for all $n\\geq 1$. Let $m\\geq 3$. So from Theorem 【50†2.3】, we derive\nL415: \nL416:  | $$D(B_{n},-m)=\\bigl(m(m-2)\\bigr)^{n}(1-2m)+m^{2}(m-1)^{2n}-2(-m)^{n}.$$  |\nL417: \nL418: For odd $n$, we have\nL419: \nL420:  | $$D(B_{n},-m)=-(2m-1)m^{n}(m-2)^{n}+m^{2}(m-1)^{2n}+2m^{n}.$$  |\nL421: As $(m-1)^{2}=m(m-2)+1>m(m-2),$ so we obtain\nL422: \nL423:  | $$m^{2}(m-1)^{2n}>m^{2}\\bigl(m(m-2)\\bigr)^{n}=m^{n+2}(m-2)^{n}.$$  |\nL424: \nL425: Thus, it follows that\nL426: \nL427:  | $$D(B_{n},-m)>m^{n}(m-2)^{n}\\bigl(m^{2}-(2m-1)\\bigr)+2m^{n}m^{n}(m-2)^{n}(m-1)^{2}+2m^{n}>0.$$  |\nL428: \nL429: For even $n$, we have\nL430: \nL431:  | $$D(B_{n},-m)=-(2m-1)m^{n}(m-2)^{n}+m^{2}(m-1)^{2n}-2m^{n}.$$  |\nL432: \nL433: With inequality ideas as above, we have\nL434: \nL435:  | $$D(B_{n},-m)>m^{n}(m-2)^{n}\\bigl(m^{2}-(2m-1)\\bigr)-2m^{n}m^{n}\\Bigl((m-1)^{2}(m-2)^{n}-2\\Bigr).$$  |\nL436: Given that $m\\geq 3$, it follows that $(m-1)^{2}(m-2)^{n}\\geq 4$, so the final expression is positive. Therefore, $D(B_{n},-m)>0$ for all $m\\geq 3$. Consequently, no negative number, except potentially $0$, can serve as a domination root. We ascertain that $0$ is the only integer domination root of $B_{n}$. ∎\nL437: \nL438: Next, we have the following consequence related to the integer domination roof for friendship and the book graph.\nL439: ###### Corollary 6.3.\nL440: \nL441: Within the graph families $\\{F_{n}:n\\geq 1\\}\\cup\\{B_{n}:n\\geq 1\\}$ the only integer domination root is $0$. Moreover, by Theorem 3.4 of [【29†7】], the corona-type families $B_{2m-1}\\circ F_{2m-1},B_{2m}\\circ F_{2m+1},$ and $B_{2m+1}\\circ F_{2m}$ have $-2$ as their only nonzero real domination root. Consequently, among the natural families presently understood, the only nonzero integer domination root that actually occurs is $-2$.\nL442: Proof. The first statement follows from Theorems 【57†6.1】 and 【58†6.2】. The second statement is exactly the content of the cited corona-family theorem from [【29†7】]. Since every integer root is real, the final conclusion follows. ∎\nL443: ###### Open Problem 6.4.\nL444: \nL445: The global problem remains open: is $-2$ the only possible nonzero integer domination root of an arbitrary graph?\nL446: Question 3.6 in [【29†7】] was articulated for arbitrary graphs. Theorems 【57†6.1】 and 【58†6.2】 demonstrate that two essential dense families, friendship graphs and book graphs, provide no nonzero integer roots whatsoever. Corollary 【59†6.3】 subsequently integrates these findings with established corona constructs to iden